# ============================ ACCOUNTS 3 ============================
LESSONS_A3 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Bank reconciliation: what it is and why monthly", "11", "<p>This module covers the banking layer of ZhiftERP: setting up bank accounts and payment modes, importing bank statements, the Bank Reconciliation Tool, handling the differences, cash and the imprest float, the retail cash-up chain, and bank governance.</p><p><b>The idea.</b> The bank statement is a record the business does not write. Internal records can share one fault — the same wrong default poisons the invoice and the register together — but the statement states what money actually moved, when, and where. Reconciliation is the monthly comparison of the two records: every statement line matched to a ledger entry, every ledger bank entry matched to a statement line, and the unmatched remainder named and worked.</p><p><b>What reconciliation is not.</b></p><p>• Not a formality. A tick that examines nothing proves nothing.<br>• Not a plug. A balancing Journal Entry that forces agreement hides every real difference. Chapter 9 treats the plug as this module's most serious failure.<br>• Not optional at any size. The cost is about an hour per bank per month once the routine is established.</p><p><b>What an unreconciled business cannot know.</b></p><p>1. Whether its cash position is real. Decisions made on a GL bank balance the bank disagrees with are decisions made on a wrong number.<br>2. Whether payments landed — the transfer that failed silently, the cheque never presented.<br>3. What the bank has been taking — charges and deductions accumulating unbooked.<br>4. Whether anyone is helping themselves. The common frauds — the intercepted payment, the altered beneficiary, takings banked short — are caught first at reconciliation or not at all.</p><p><b>How ZhiftERP does it.</b> The system's reconciliation is built from four parts, each with its chapter: the <b>Bank Account</b> record linked to a Bank-type ledger account (Chapter 2); the <b>Bank Statement Import</b>, which loads statement lines as <b>Bank Transaction</b> records (Chapter 3); the <b>Bank Reconciliation Tool</b>, which matches Bank Transactions against Payment Entries and Journal Entries (Chapter 3); and the <b>Bank Reconciliation Statement</b> report, which states the GL balance, the bank balance, and the explained difference between them (Chapter 4).</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: bank accounts and Modes of Payment. Chapter 3: importing the statement and matching. Chapter 4: working the differences. Chapter 5: cash accounts and the imprest float. Chapter 6: the cash-up chain from till to statement. Chapter 7: bank governance. Chapter 8: the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Bank accounts and Modes of Payment", "12", "<p>Reconciliation needs the setup right first. Three records per real bank account, walked in order.</p><p><b>1. The ledger account.</b> On the Chart of Accounts, create one leaf per real bank account under Bank Accounts, with <b>Account Type = Bank</b>. Name it with the bank and account identity — <i>GTB Current - 0123</i> — so every report reads plainly for years. Two rules follow:</p><p>• Never pool two real accounts into one leaf. Two statements cannot reconcile against one ledger.<br>• Never split one real account across leaves.</p><p><b>2. The Bank Account record.</b> Open <b>Accounting &gt; Bank Account &gt; New</b>. Fill: Account Name, Bank (create the Bank record once per bank), Account Number, <b>Is Company Account</b> ticked, Company, and — the linkage that matters — the <b>Account</b> field pointed at the ledger leaf from step 1. The Bank Account record is what the statement import and the reconciliation tool work from; a leaf without its Bank Account record is invisible to both.</p><p><b>3. Modes of Payment.</b> Open <b>Accounting &gt; Mode of Payment</b>. Each mode — Cash, Transfer, POS, Cheque — carries an Accounts table mapping the mode, per company, to the ledger account the money truly lands in: Transfer to the receiving bank leaf, Cash to the cash account, POS to its settlement account. A mis-mapped mode makes every payment taken on it post to the wrong leaf: one bank's reconciliation fills with entries the statement will never show, and another's misses entries the statement does show. Verify the mapping at go-live by posting one test Payment Entry per mode and reading View &gt; Accounting Ledger; re-check whenever a bank is added or changed.</p><p><b>Movements between your own accounts.</b> Cash banked, funds moved between banks, the float topped up. Record these either as a <b>Payment Entry with Payment Type = Internal Transfer</b> (from-account and to-account on one document) or as a <b>Journal Entry with Entry Type = Contra Entry</b>. Nothing posts to income or expense. Both sides land on a reconcilable record: the sending side matches when the money leaves that statement, the receiving side when it arrives, and a transfer initiated Friday that lands Monday shows as an in-transit item both reconciliations can see. Chapter 4 sets the age limit on it.</p><p><b>Multiple banks.</b> Businesses hold several accounts for real reasons — clearing, lending, payroll. Write each account's purpose in the Bank Account record's notes (operating, tax reserve, payroll), and review the list annually: every account should still have a job.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> One typed leaf per real account; a Bank Account record linking each leaf to its bank and number; every Mode of Payment mapped and test-verified; internal movements by Internal Transfer or Contra Entry; purposes written down.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Importing the statement and matching", "13", "<p>The monthly reconciliation in ZhiftERP is two steps: load the statement, then match it. Run it per bank account, from the statement's own cutoff date.</p><p><b>Step 1 — load the statement.</b> Open <b>Bank Statement Import</b> (search it from the top bar). Select the Bank Account, upload the bank's file (CSV or XLSX export from the bank portal), and map the columns — date, description, deposit, withdrawal, reference — the first time; the mapping is remembered. On import, each statement line becomes a <b>Bank Transaction</b> record with status Unreconciled. If a bank offers no clean export, lines can be entered as Bank Transactions manually, but push for the export: typing a statement re-introduces the typing errors the import exists to avoid.</p><p><b>Step 2 — match in the Bank Reconciliation Tool.</b> Open <b>Accounting &gt; Bank Reconciliation Tool</b>. Select the Bank Account and the period; the screen shows the closing balance per the bank and per the books, and lists the unreconciled Bank Transactions. Work each transaction with its action buttons:</p><p>• <b>Match Against Voucher</b> — the normal case: the tool proposes Payment Entries and Journal Entries with matching amounts; confirm the right one. Matching sets the voucher's <b>clearance date</b>, which is what marks a ledger entry as seen by the bank.<br>• One statement line covering several vouchers (a batched deposit), or one voucher split across lines: select multiple documents in the match dialog until the amounts agree.<br>• <b>Create Voucher</b> — for statement lines with no ledger entry yet: bank charges, interest, direct debits. The tool creates the Payment Entry or Journal Entry from the transaction, with the statement line as its reference. This is the only lawful source for booking bank-side items — never book charges from memory.<br>• A transaction that matches nothing and should not create anything — the bank's own error — is left unreconciled and queried with the bank until reversed.</p><p><b>Matching order.</b> Run easiest to hardest: exact amount-and-date pairs first (the bulk), then amount matches with date drift (the payment entered Tuesday, on the statement Thursday — match it; the clearance date records the lag), then the composites, and last the remainder that will not match — which is not a failure but the month's worklist, handled in Chapter 4.</p><p><b>The proof.</b> After matching, open the <b>Bank Reconciliation Statement</b> report for the account and date: it shows the GL balance, adds cheques and payments not yet cleared, and arrives at the bank balance. When that report closes with every listed item named and expected, the account is reconciled. When it closes only through an unexplained difference, it is not.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Working the differences", "13", "<p>Matching leaves a remainder on both sides. Each item belongs to one of three categories, and each category has a procedure.</p><p><b>Category 1 — timing items (book side).</b> Entries in the ledger the bank has not yet seen: the cheque issued and unpresented, the transfer in transit, the deposit lodged after cutoff. These appear on the Bank Reconciliation Statement as uncleared items and normally clear next month. The discipline is a written <b>age limit per instrument</b>:</p><p>• <b>Cheques</b> — the validity window. A cheque unpresented at six months is stale; resolve it: contact the payee, and if the cheque is lost, reverse the Payment Entry by module 2's correction rules and reissue properly.<br>• <b>Transfers</b> — days, not months. In transit beyond a week means missing: trace it with the bank and find or force the reversal.<br>• <b>Deposits</b> — a lodged deposit missing from the statement is escalated the <b>same day</b>. Chapter 6's chain shows who carried it.</p><p>Read the uncleared list monthly with its ages. A remainder list that only grows is Chapter 9's Pattern 3 starting.</p><p><b>Category 2 — bank-side items, unbooked.</b> Lines on the statement with no ledger entry: charges, interest, direct debits, standing orders, reversals, a customer paying straight into the account. Book each from the Bank Reconciliation Tool's <b>Create Voucher</b> button, statement line referenced. Two rules ride along:</p><p>1. <b>Read charges before booking them.</b> Compare against the bank's agreed schedule quarterly. Banks make errors, and a business that books blindly pays for them. Query and recover duplicates.<br>2. <b>An unexplained credit is not income.</b> Park it against a holding account, investigate the payer — it is usually a customer paying without a reference — and allocate it through the receivables tools (module 4) so the customer's ledger stays correct.</p><p><b>Category 3 — errors.</b> Book-side errors surface here constantly: the transposed amount, the payment posted to the wrong bank leaf, the double entry. Correct each by module 2's rules — Reverse Journal Entry or cancel/amend the Payment Entry, with references and remarks. Bank-side errors are queried with the bank and tracked until reversed; never absorb them.</p><p><b>The monthly output.</b> Finish every reconciliation with two artifacts filed: the Bank Reconciliation Statement report (printed or saved to the close bundle) and the aged uncleared list with an owner per item. The list's length and age are the reconciliation's quality measures.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Timing items ride under written age limits — cheque by validity, transfer by the week, missing deposit same day. Bank-side items book only through Create Voucher with the statement referenced. Errors correct by the rules. File the statement report and the aged list every month.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Cash accounts and the imprest float", "12", "<p>The statement covers electronic money. Physical cash has no outside record at all, so its rules are stricter.</p><p><b>Cash accounts: few and owned.</b> Create a <b>Cash-type</b> leaf for each real cash custody — the main cash account, each petty cash float. Do not run one shared Cash account that several drawers post to: when several people share one balance, no variance can be attributed to anyone. Each custody gets a named holder, a counting schedule, and a written purpose. Design for minimisation: Nigeria's transfer-heavy payments make it practical to keep drawers few, floats small, and banking fast.</p><p><b>The imprest float, set up once.</b></p><p>1. Create the float's Cash-type account, e.g. <i>Petty Cash - Head Office</i>, and fund it to a fixed amount — say ₦100,000 — by an Internal Transfer from the bank.<br>2. Name the custodian. Disbursements happen only against a <b>voucher</b>: what, why, who, receipt attached. The vouchers accumulate in the box; nothing posts yet.<br>3. <b>Replenish monthly:</b> one Journal Entry (Entry Type: Cash Entry) debits each voucher's expense account with its Cost Center, credits the bank for the vouchers' exact total — restoring the float to ₦100,000. Attach the voucher scans to the entry.</p><p>The invariant that makes the float self-checking: <b>cash in the box plus vouchers in the box equals ₦100,000 at every moment.</b> A spot count takes two minutes and either ties or tells.</p><p><b>The cash count.</b> Scheduled counts (the float monthly; tills daily — Chapter 6) plus unscheduled spot counts, because unpredictability is what keeps a counting programme honest. Count against the invariant, preferably by someone other than the custodian. Variance rules are the standard set: named the same day, investigated before adjusted, reason-coded, and tracked as a rate per custody. Frame the counts correctly with the team: the count protects the honest holder — a clean-count record is the custodian's own evidence.</p><p><b>Cash rules, itemised.</b></p><p>• Disbursements only against vouchers. No IOUs in the box — an IOU is an unauthorised loan on a scrap of paper.<br>• The float is fixed. Topping up by feel breaks the invariant.<br>• A spending cap routes anything substantial to a proper Payment Entry, where it gets approval and evidence.<br>• Custodian changes are a counted handover: outgoing count, incoming count, both signed, same day.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> One Cash-type account per custody with a named holder; the imprest cycle — fixed float, vouchers, exact replenishment by one Cash Entry with cost centres; scheduled plus spot counts against the invariant; and the four cash rules.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The cash-up chain: till to statement", "13", "<p>Retail cash runs through a four-link chain from the till drawer to the bank statement. Each link produces a number, the numbers must agree, and each link has a named custodian. The till mechanics themselves belong to the ZhiftPOS track; this chapter is the accounts view of the chain.</p><p><b>Link 1 — the till session.</b> Each till, each day or shift, is one session: opened with a counted float, traded through the till (every sale a document), closed with a counted drawer. One till, one operator, one session — a pooled drawer shared by several operators turns every variance into an argument, so it is refused.</p><p><b>Link 2 — the cash-up.</b> At session close:</p><p>1. Count the drawer <b>by denomination</b> on a count sheet. \"Roughly ₦180k\" is not a count.<br>2. Compare against the session's system totals <b>by mode</b>: cash sales against cash in the drawer less the float; POS-terminal sales against the terminal's settlement report; transfers against the transfer log.<br>3. Name any variance <b>the same day</b>. A ₦14,000 short investigated at close is usually a training catch; found a week later it is an argument; a month later, a write-off.</p><p>Track cash-up variances as a rate per till and per operator. Rotating operators across tills separates the till from the person when a pattern appears; a clean-session streak is the operator's own record.</p><p><b>Link 3 — banking the takings.</b></p><p>• <b>Intact:</b> the whole count is banked — never the count minus informal spendings. Spendings out of the drawer break the tie between till total and deposit slip and skip all documentation; route them through the float or a Payment Entry instead.<br>• <b>Fast:</b> daily or next morning per policy; the overnight safe holds the minimum.<br>• The deposit is carried by a named person, and the deposit slip must equal the cash-up's banked figure to the naira. Record the banking as an Internal Transfer from the cash account to the bank account.</p><p><b>Link 4 — the statement.</b> The deposit appears on the imported statement and is matched in the Bank Reconciliation Tool (Chapter 3). The full chain — till total → cash-up sheet → deposit slip → statement line — is one sequence of agreeing numbers. A break names its own link: till-to-cash-up is a session problem; cash-up-to-slip happened in counting or carriage; slip-to-statement is Chapter 4's same-day deposit alarm.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> One operator per session; denominated counts compared by mode with same-day variances; takings banked intact and fast by named carriage, recorded as Internal Transfers; the statement match closing the loop monthly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Bank governance", "12", "<p>Bank money moves at transfer speed, so its controls are the strictest in the system. Four sets, each a procedure.</p><p><b>1. Prepare and release, separated.</b> The person who assembles a payment run (from approved invoices and matched documents in the system) is never the person who authorises it at the bank. Two people, at any size: at eleven staff, the founder releases what the accountant prepares. The run pays what the ledger supports — never what a message asks.</p><p><b>2. Masters, not messages.</b> Payments go to the bank details stored in the <b>Supplier record</b> — never to details supplied in an email or chat. Changes to a supplier's bank details are a governed master-data event: verified through a known channel back to a known person at the supplier — never the phone number given in the requesting message. The urgent email from the \"MD\", the lookalike supplier domain, the convincing WhatsApp — each one dies against the process, which is the point of having one. The accounts team is this fraud's chosen target; refresh the training yearly and treat reported near-misses as wins.</p><p><b>3. Mandates and users.</b> The bank-side mirror of system permissions:</p><p>• The bank mandate — who signs, at what thresholds, in what combinations — is kept aligned with the internal release design. Broader than the internal control is a back door; narrower is a bottleneck.<br>• Review the mandate annually <b>and at every personnel change</b>. A departed signatory is removed the week they leave.<br>• Online banking gets the same list: users named, limits set, tokens held personally, admin rights held by two named people.</p><p><b>4. The bank calendar.</b></p><p>• <b>Monthly:</b> reconciliation per account, with a written maximum unreconciled age — an account two months unreconciled is treated as an incident, not a backlog. The aged uncleared list read.<br>• <b>Quarterly:</b> the bank-charges read against the agreed schedule.<br>• <b>Annually and on change:</b> the mandate and online-user review.<br>• <b>Per schedule:</b> the cash counts (Chapter 5) and till sessions (Chapter 6).<br>• <b>Daily, where cash is tight:</b> the cash position across accounts. The Bank Account dashboard and the General Ledger report by account serve this read.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Preparer never releases; beneficiaries paid from the Supplier record with verified changes; mandates aligned, reviewed, and purged the week people leave; the calendar — monthly reconciliation with a maximum age, quarterly charges read, annual mandate walk.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's banking year", "13", "<p>HealthTrade's banking layer, installed and run. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>Setup and the first reconciliation (Chapters 2-4).</b> Three banks become three Bank-type leaves, each with its Bank Account record and written purpose — operating, tax reserve, payroll. The Mode of Payment mappings are test-verified (module 1's check had already caught one). Then the first reconciliation of the operating account, facing eighteen months of never-reconciled history. The statement files are imported month by month through Bank Statement Import, matched in the Bank Reconciliation Tool, and the remainder — <b>34 items</b> — is worked by category:</p><p>• A nine-month-old <b>stale cheque</b>: ₦210,000 to a supplier who never presented it. The payee is contacted (the cheque was lost), the Payment Entry reversed with references, the payment reissued through the run.<br>• Fourteen months of <b>unbooked charges</b>, booked in one sitting through Create Voucher with each statement line referenced. The quarterly charges read then finds two duplicate charges; the bank refunds <b>₦38,000</b>.<br>• One <b>mis-mapped POS week</b> — settlements posted to the wrong bank leaf during a terminal change. Corrected by the rules; the Mode of Payment mapping fixed.<br>• An <b>unexplained credit</b> of ₦127,000 — parked, traced within the week to a customer who paid without a reference, allocated through Payment Reconciliation (module 4) so the customer's ledger stayed correct.</p><p>Month two's reconciliation takes fifty minutes; month three's, forty.</p><p><b>The float (Chapter 5).</b> The office drawer becomes a ₦100,000 imprest float: Cash-type account, named custodian, voucher pad, monthly replenishment by one Cash Entry with cost centres, voucher scans attached. The first unscheduled spot count in week three ties to the naira — cash plus vouchers equals one hundred — and the custodian asks when the next one is. Unscheduled, is the answer.</p><p><b>The cash-up chain (Chapter 6).</b> Till sessions start at the retail counter. Day nine, the evening count is <b>₦14,000 short</b> against cash sales — named that night, investigated while memory serves, resolved as two transfer-paid sales rung as cash by a new operator. A mode error, not a theft: the sessions corrected by document, the operator trained, the variance rate back at zero. Takings bank intact next morning by named carriage as Internal Transfers; slips match cash-ups; the statement matches the slips.</p><p><b>Governance (Chapter 7).</b> The mandate review finds a former manager still a signatory eight months after leaving — removed the same week, and the trigger added to the leaver checklist. Prepare/release is confirmed live: the accountant prepares, the MD releases. By year-end the three banks reconcile in under two hours a month total, and the MD's note: <i>the books and the bank now settle their differences monthly, and I read the result.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the third law", "11", "<p>The banking layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the never-reconciled account.</b> Symptom: a GL bank balance nobody would rely on; decisions made on it anyway; the statement opened only to check if one payment landed. Cause: the comparison never performed (Chapters 1, 3). Fix: the first-dig reconciliation — it faces the whole backlog once; import the history through Bank Statement Import and work it — then the monthly routine, with the written maximum unreconciled age making relapse an incident.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — reconciliation by plug.</b> Symptom: accounts that \"reconcile\" perfectly every month via a balancing Journal Entry to a differences or suspense account. Cause: forcing agreement converts every real difference — error, fraud, drift — into a booked fiction; the account looks reconciled precisely because it never is. This is the module's most serious failure. Fix: excavate the plug account's history (each plugged amount is an unworked difference), forbid the plug, and install the name-every-item rule. An unexplained difference carried openly on the Bank Reconciliation Statement is honest; one booked away is not.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the museum remainder.</b> Symptom: uncleared items riding for a year; stale cheques never resolved; in-transit items months old. Cause: no age limits (Chapter 4). Fix: the limits written per instrument, the monthly aged read with investigation at the limit, and the list's length and age on the close checklist.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — cash counted never.</b> Symptom: a drawer nobody can explain; IOUs in the float; petty cash topped up by feel; variances found annually and absorbed. Cause: the no-record asset given no rules either (Chapter 5). Fix: the imprest installed — the invariant makes counting trivial — custodies named and minimal, the count schedule with spot checks, the spending cap routing real expenses to Payment Entries.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the leaking chain.</b> Symptom: till totals, cash-up sheets, deposit slips and statement lines each showing a slightly different number; takings banked late and light. Cause: the chain's links unsigned (Chapter 6) — pooled tills, undenominated counts, informal spendings en route, unnamed carriage. Fix: one operator per session, same-day denominated cash-ups, intact and fast banking recorded as Internal Transfers, and the four-link match run at every reconciliation.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: set up the three records per bank (typed leaf, Bank Account record, Mode of Payment mapping) and verify the mapping; run Bank Statement Import and match in the Bank Reconciliation Tool, including Create Voucher for bank-side items; read the Bank Reconciliation Statement report; state the three remainder categories with their age limits; set up and replenish an imprest float and state its invariant; walk the four links of the cash-up chain and what each break means; and state the governance set — prepare/release, masters-not-messages, mandate reviews, and the calendar.</p><p><b>The third law: every bank account is reconciled monthly, and every difference is named — never plugged.</b> Module 4 covers receivables and payables from the accounts side.</p>"),
]

