# ============================ ACCOUNTS 4 ============================
LESSONS_A4 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The AR/AP layer and the tie check", "11", "<p>This module covers running receivables and payables from the accounts side: the reports, the Payment Reconciliation tool, advances, provisioning and write-offs, customer statements, opening balances, and the month-end routine.</p><p><b>The division of work.</b> The sales desk chases customers; the purchasing desk pays suppliers. The accounts side owns the <b>record</b>: that the Debtors and Creditors figures are real, correctly aged, honestly valued, and complete. A receivable has no shelf to count — its entire existence is the party's ledger, the documents behind it, and the agreement between the control account and its parties.</p><p><b>The four questions.</b> Read the receivables balance as four claims to verify:</p><p>1. <b>Real</b> — every naira attached to a Party and backed by documents.<br>2. <b>Collectable</b> — the provision question: ₦18.4m gross of which ₦1.2m is doubtful is a different figure.<br>3. <b>Current</b> — the aging honest, advances not netted against debts (Chapter 3).<br>4. <b>Complete</b> — nothing deleted, nothing parked off the ledger.</p><p>An external audit's receivables work is these four questions asked systematically. Keep them answered all year and the audit is short.</p><p><b>The tie check, as a procedure.</b> Module 1 introduced it; this module runs it monthly, first, always:</p><p>1. Run the <b>Trial Balance</b>; note the Debtors closing balance.<br>2. Run the <b>Accounts Receivable</b> report as at the same date; note its grand total.<br>3. They must agree to the kobo. Repeat for Creditors against <b>Accounts Payable</b>.<br>4. On a difference: run the General Ledger report on the control account filtered to <b>Voucher Type = Journal Entry</b>. The cause is almost always there. Correct it by module 2's rules and never carry a divergence forward unexplained.</p><p><b>The two reports, introduced.</b> <b>Accounts Receivable</b> (Accounting &gt; Accounts Receivable): every customer's open items in aging buckets, based on due date — the collections worklist and the aging evidence. <b>Accounts Payable</b> mirrors it for suppliers. Both accept Party, Cost Center, and ageing-basis filters; the Summary versions give one row per party. These two reports, plus the Payment Reconciliation tool in Chapter 2, carry most of this module's work.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: allocation and the Payment Reconciliation tool. Chapter 3: advances, both directions. Chapter 4: provisioning and write-offs. Chapter 5: statements and confirmations. Chapter 6: opening balances. Chapter 7: the month-end routine. Chapter 8: the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Allocation and the Payment Reconciliation tool", "12", "<p>A party's ledger is entries; a party's readable story is entries <b>matched</b> — this payment against these invoices, this credit note applied to that invoice. Every unmatched item is ambiguity, and this chapter is the tool and routine that clear it.</p><p><b>What unallocated items do.</b></p><p>• <b>A payment on account</b> (received with no invoice references): the invoices it should have cleared still age as overdue in the Accounts Receivable report, while the customer's net balance looks fine. The aging now overstates, and collections may chase paid debts.<br>• <b>A credit note issued but never applied:</b> the customer's statement shows a debt and a floating credit side by side; the customer pays the net and disputes the rest.<br>• <b>An advance never matched to its later invoice</b> — Chapter 3's subject.</p><p>The balance can be right while the story is unreadable, and unreadable stories cost collections calls, statement disputes, and eventually the relationship.</p><p><b>The Payment Reconciliation tool.</b> Open <b>Accounting &gt; Payment Reconciliation</b>. Select the Company, the Party Type and Party, and the receivable/payable account. The screen loads two lists: unallocated payments and credit notes on one side, open invoices on the other. Select the items to match, adjust the allocation amounts where one payment covers several invoices, and click <b>Reconcile</b>. The tool writes the allocation — the invoices close, the payment stops floating, the aging corrects — without any new GL posting, because it is matching existing entries, not creating new ones.</p><p><b>The monthly unallocated read, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. Run Accounts Receivable and filter or scan for negative and unallocated rows; do the same on the payables side.<br>2. For each party with floating items, open Payment Reconciliation and match. Where the customer's intent is unknown, ask them: <i>your payment of the 14th — against which invoices?</i> A normal, professional question, which also signals that the account is watched.<br>3. Genuine overpayments are refunded by a proper Payment Entry (Pay, referencing the credit), not left to confuse the aging.<br>4. Items nobody can explain after honest work are escalated by name — rare, and exactly what this routine exists to surface.</p><p><b>Why near-zero matters.</b> Kept near zero: every statement is self-explanatory, every aging line is a real debt, and the monthly tie check is trivial. Lapsed for two quarters: the aging overstates, collections chase paid debts, statements teach customers to dispute, and the cleanup becomes a project. Grade each month's allocation discipline by this list's length — it costs one glance.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Advances, both directions", "13", "<p>Money moves ahead of documents in both directions: customers pay before invoices exist, and the business pays suppliers before bills arrive. Each direction has its handling.</p><p><b>Customer advances — a liability.</b> Cash received ahead of earning is not income and not a negative receivable left to distort the aging. It is a liability: the business owes goods, services, or the money back.</p><p>1. <b>Capture it as an advance.</b> On the Payment Entry, when there is no invoice to reference, the amount stays unallocated — and where the order exists, receive the payment against the Sales Order so it is recorded as an advance on that order. The system posts the advance to the configured liability treatment rather than letting it float as a mystery credit.<br>2. <b>Match it when the invoice books.</b> The Sales Invoice's Advances section pulls the advance in (Get Advances Received), consuming the advance and settling the invoice in one step. An advance left floating after its trade completed is Chapter 2's most expensive case: the customer shows a debt and a credit nobody joined — and pays neither attention.<br>3. <b>Report it as what it is</b> — the never-net rule below.</p><p><b>Supplier advances — an asset, and a known trap.</b> The advance paid to a supplier is a prepayment asset: pay it against the Purchase Order (the order's Advance Paid field tracks it), and pull it into the Purchase Invoice's Advances section when the bill lands. The trap this defends against: the advance paid, the goods arriving, and the bill settled <b>again</b> in full because nobody matched — the double payment. Two rules:</p><p>1. <b>Age the advances.</b> Review supplier advances monthly against expected delivery dates. An advance aging past its delivery is either a late supplier (an expediting conversation) or a claim quietly going stale.<br>2. <b>Check for open advances before settlement.</b> The invoice-entry step pulls advances before the payment run sees the bill; make this a workflow habit and audit it.</p><p><b>The never-net rule.</b> Do not report customer advances netted inside the receivables line. ₦19.6m owed to the business and ₦1.2m owed by it are two different figures — an asset and a liability, with different risks and different tax treatment — not \"₦18.4m receivable\". The statements show them separately, always. The test: can a banker see the real position from the face of the statement?</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Customer advances: captured against the order, consumed through the invoice's Advances section, never left floating. Supplier advances: paid against the order, pulled into the bill, aged monthly, checked before every settlement. Both directions reported un-netted.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Provisioning and write-offs", "13", "<p>Some debts will not be collected, and the books must say so before the auditor asks. Two stages, each with its procedure.</p><p><b>Stage 1 — doubtful: the provision.</b> When collection is uncertain — the customer silent past every escalation, disputing without engaging, visibly in distress — the response is a provision: an estimate of the loss, charged now, while the debt <b>stays on the customer's ledger</b> and collections keeps working it.</p><p>• The entry: a Journal Entry — debit Bad Debt Expense, credit <b>Provision for Doubtful Debts</b> (create this account as a leaf under receivables' group so it reports against gross receivables).<br>• The size: by a written <b>aging matrix</b>, not by mood. Percentages per aging bucket, built from the business's own collection history — for example nil to 60 days, 10% at 61-120, 30% at 121-180, 60% at 181-365, 100% past a year. The Accounts Receivable report's buckets feed the computation directly.<br>• Each period, the matrix is run against the fresh aging and one Journal Entry adjusts the provision <b>to</b> the required level — up as a charge, down as a release. Keep the matrix stable (changed annually, as an event): then a provision movement means collections actually changed, not that someone turned a dial. Specific cases override the matrix in both directions, documented: the large debt with specific bad news provided fully regardless of age; the aged debt on a performing payment plan relieved with a note.</p><p><b>Stage 2 — bad: the write-off.</b> When collection has definitively failed — the customer wound up, vanished, or pursuit costs more than the claim per policy — the debt leaves the ledger:</p><p>1. Evidence assembled: the chase history and dossier.<br>2. Approval on the <b>two-signature path</b> — configure the write-off Journal Entry (or the invoice's write-off) behind a Workflow with value routing.<br>3. The entry posts against the provision (or straight to expense where unprovided), with the tax notes captured — deductibility has conditions the accountant owns.<br>4. <b>The customer record is retained</b>, never deleted: written off is not erased, and the history matters the day the customer returns asking for credit.</p><p><b>Recoveries.</b> A written-off debt sometimes pays. Receive it as a normal Payment Entry referencing the story in its remarks; it lands as income — and it lands cleanly precisely because the customer record survived the write-off. Chapter 8 shows HealthTrade's own case.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Doubtful: provision by matrix Journal Entry, debt stays, collections continues. Bad: two-signature write-off with evidence and tax notes, record retained. Matrix stable, movements explained, recoveries through the normal payment machinery.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Statements and confirmations", "12", "<p>The party ledger faces the outside world through four routines.</p><p><b>1. The statement run — automated.</b> The customer statement is the party ledger, printed. ZhiftERP automates the run with <b>Process Statement Of Accounts</b> (search it from the top bar): select the customers (or a customer group), the period, the format (with or without aging), and either print the PDFs or — the useful part — schedule the tool to <b>email each customer their statement monthly</b>, automatically. Set it up once at go-live. The run returns three things:</p><p>• Collections pressure — the statement is the chase's standing reminder.<br>• Free error-checking — customers audit their statements: the missed payment, the unapplied credit, the invoice they never received all come back as queries, each one a correction you did not have to find yourself.<br>• Dispute hygiene — disagreements handled on record: the disputed invoice flagged in the system, the resolution documented, and the correcting instrument (a Credit Note from the invoice's Return button, an allocation in Payment Reconciliation, or a firm restatement of the debt) issued as a document. Never side-agreements in someone's inbox — a side-agreement contradicts every statement printed after it.</p><p><b>2. The audit confirmation.</b> The auditor selects customers, sends balance confirmations, and compares replies to the ledger. A business that runs this module meets it with the four questions pre-answered: balances party-attached and documented (real), provisions on the matrix (collectable), advances un-netted (current), nothing deleted (complete). One courtesy shortens it: warn the selected customers that confirmations are coming — an expected confirmation gets a reply.</p><p><b>3. The supplier statement reconciliation — the mirror.</b> The payables side has its own external record: the <b>supplier's</b> statement of your account, reconciled against your ledger (the General Ledger report filtered to the supplier, or the Accounts Payable report). Match their lines to your entries and name the remainder. What it catches:</p><p>• Invoices they show that you never received — a liability found before it arrives as a surprise demand.<br>• Credit notes they issued that you never captured — money owed to you, unclaimed.<br>• Payments you made that they never applied — the allocation conversation, from the other side.</p><p>Run it monthly for the top suppliers, on rotation for the rest.</p><p><b>4. Dispute records.</b> Keep every dispute inside the system: the flagged document, the correspondence attached, the resolving instrument linked. Six months later the story reads itself.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Statements by Process Statement Of Accounts on a monthly schedule; disputes resolved on record by document instruments; confirmations met pre-answered; supplier statements reconciled — top names monthly, the rest on rotation.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Opening balances and migration", "13", "<p>Every AR/AP ledger is born in a migration, and how it is loaded decides whether the tie check agrees from day one or never has.</p><p><b>The loading rule: detail, never totals.</b> Opening receivables load <b>per customer, per open invoice</b> — each balance decomposed into its actual open invoices with their numbers, dates, amounts, and due dates from the old records. Three things depend on it:</p><p>1. The Accounts Receivable report's aging is true from day one — every balance ages from its real invoice date, not from go-live.<br>2. Every future payment has real invoices to allocate against — Chapter 2's routine is possible at all.<br>3. The tie is born intact — the control equals the sum of real party detail.</p><p>The alternative — one opening balance per customer, or one Journal Entry to the Debtors control — produces a blind aging (collections priorities scrambled for a year), payments on account forever, and a control with no details. The same rule mirrors to payables: per supplier, per open bill.</p><p><b>The tool.</b> Use the <b>Opening Invoice Creation Tool</b> (search it from the top bar): choose Sales or Purchase, and enter one row per open invoice — party, invoice number, date, amount, due date. The tool generates opening invoices marked <b>Is Opening = Yes</b>, posting against the Temporary Opening account so year one fabricates no revenue or expense. For businesses with clean export files, prepare the rows in the tool's template and load in batches.</p><p><b>The identity rules riding along.</b></p><p>• <b>True dates where possible.</b> Where the system's opening pattern forces a posting-date compromise, preserve the due dates — the aging runs on them. The accountant's call, documented.<br>• <b>Advances load as advances</b> — unallocated payments and order-linked advances, never netted into balances. Chapter 3's rule applies from the first day.<br>• <b>Disputed balances load flagged</b>, with the dispute history summarised — migration does not launder a dispute.<br>• <b>The opening provision loads too:</b> the matrix applied to the opening aging, posted as the policy's opening position — the honesty built in at birth, not retrofitted at the first year-end.</p><p><b>The migration reconciliation.</b> Before go-live is signed off:</p><p>1. The new system's Debtors and Creditors balances tie to the old system's (or the signed trial balance's) totals, to the kobo.<br>2. The new Accounts Receivable and Payable report totals tie to the old party listings.<br>3. Differences — there are always some — are worked to zero or documented as deliberate corrections: the old system's known errors fixed at migration, listed, and signed.</p><p>Practical note: the detailed load costs days where the total-per-party load costs hours. Price it honestly at planning — the cheap load's real price is a year of blind aging and a tie that never agreed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The month-end AR/AP routine", "12", "<p>The module's routines, assembled into one monthly sitting. About ninety minutes at a healthy business, one owner, run in this order.</p><p><b>1. The tie check — first, always.</b> Trial Balance Debtors against the Accounts Receivable report total; Creditors against Accounts Payable; to the kobo. Agreement: one line, initialed — two minutes. Difference: an incident — the Journal Entry filter on the control account finds the cause; correct by the rules; never carry it forward unexplained.</p><p><b>2. The unallocated sweep.</b> Chapter 2's read: floating payments and unapplied credits per party, matched in Payment Reconciliation, refunds issued properly, the unexplainable escalated by name. Grade the month by the list's length.</p><p><b>3. The advance aging — both directions.</b> Customer advances against their expected completions; supplier advances against expected deliveries (the Purchase Orders' Advance Paid figures). Items past plausibility resolved or escalated. Confirm the reporting stays un-netted.</p><p><b>4. The provision review.</b> Run the matrix against the fresh Accounts Receivable aging; post the one adjusting Journal Entry with its one-line explanation; document any specific judgments. Write-off candidates are surfaced to the approval workflow — the routine nominates, the governance decides, never both in the same sitting.</p><p><b>5. The statement run.</b> Confirm Process Statement Of Accounts fired on schedule; work the queries that came back from last month's statements; verify open disputes are on record with their instruments.</p><p><b>6. Supplier statement reconciliations.</b> The month's tranche — top suppliers monthly, the rest on rotation. Missing bills chased, unclaimed credit notes captured.</p><p><b>7. Outputs to the close.</b> Hand upward (module 7's checklist receives them): the aged AR and AP with the provision shown, the advance schedules, the tie confirmation, the write-off log, the unallocated residue. One page each, and each already true because the routine made it so.</p><p><b>The economics.</b> Skipped for a quarter, every list doubles and the catch-up becomes a project. Kept, the year-end AR/AP work is a formality and the audit's receivables questions are pre-answered. Give the routine one owner and a fixed slot in the month — a routine with a name and a date happens; one without either is an intention.</p><p><b>Feedback to the desks.</b> The routine's findings — the recurring unallocated causes, the deduction patterns — go back to the sales and purchasing desks as process fixes, so next month's lists are shorter. The desks do not attend the sitting; they receive its output.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's year in the AR/AP chair", "13", "<p>The module's routines, installed and run at HealthTrade. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The sweep instituted (Chapter 2).</b> Month one's first unallocated read finds the expected backlog: <b>₦840,000 floating across 23 items</b> — payments on account going back nine months, four credit notes issued and never applied, two advances whose trades completed unmatched. Three afternoons in Payment Reconciliation clear most of it; customers are called with the standard question — <i>your payment of March 14th, against which invoices?</i> — one genuine overpayment is refunded by a proper Payment Entry, and one escalation (a ₦60,000 payment no record explains) resolves in week three as a sister-company transfer mis-referenced. The read closes month two at ₦45,000 and month three near zero. The corrected aging then shows <b>two genuinely overdue accounts</b> the old net balances had hidden — handed straight to collections.</p><p><b>The provision built (Chapter 4).</b> The matrix is built from two years of the business's own collection outcomes: nil to 60 days, 10% at 61-120, 30% at 121-180, 60% at 181-365, 100% past a year — written on the policy page and run against the opening aging. Opening provision: <b>₦410,000</b>, posted with its explanation to the MD: <i>this is what the aging says the book is worth; the number was always true — now it is booked.</i> Monthly movements after that are small and explained, and Q3's release reads as the good news it is, because the matrix had not moved.</p><p><b>A write-off and a recovery (Chapter 4).</b> Month five: a customer definitively gone — premises vacated, phones dead, dossier complete. <b>₦188,000</b> written off on the two-signature workflow, evidence attached, tax notes captured, the customer record retained. Month eleven: the customer's principal resurfaces, restarts smaller, and pays <b>₦120,000</b> against the old debt. The recovery is received as a normal Payment Entry, referenced end to end, and the customer's history reads whole: debt, chase, write-off, recovery, new terms — cash only. The accountant's remark: <i>the record survived the write-off, so the recovery had somewhere to land.</i></p><p><b>Statements and the mirror (Chapter 5).</b> Process Statement Of Accounts is scheduled in month one: every open-balance customer gets a monthly emailed statement with aging. The first returned queries fix two allocation gaps for free. The first supplier-statement reconciliation of the top five catches a missing bill — an invoice the supplier shows that never reached the books — and an unclaimed credit note from a returns batch: <b>₦74,000</b> recovered by asking.</p><p><b>The routine settles (Chapter 7).</b> By month six the sitting runs at its promised ninety minutes: tie checked in two, the sweep short, advances aged, one matrix entry, statements confirmed fired, the mirror's tranche done, seven one-page outputs to the close. The MD's year note: <i>the receivables number used to be an opinion. Now it is four answered questions.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the fourth law", "11", "<p>The AR/AP layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the diverged tie.</b> Symptom: the Trial Balance control disagrees with the Accounts Receivable report; nobody knows since when; reports quote whichever number suits. Cause: the tie never checked — journals to controls, total-per-party migrations, deletions (Chapters 1, 6). Fix: the Journal Entry filter finds the causes; date the divergence by working backwards; correct by the rules; and the monthly check makes the next divergence a one-month problem instead of a three-year one.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the netting error.</b> Symptom: receivables that shrink when advances arrive; statements customers dispute on sight; a balance-sheet figure nobody can decompose. Cause: customer advances crushed into the receivables line (Chapter 3). Fix: advances captured against orders and consumed through the invoice's Advances section; reporting un-netted; and the banker's-view test applied to the statements.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the perpetual gross.</b> Symptom: receivables at face value for years; aged debts immortal; the write-off unknown as an instrument. Cause: no provision policy (Chapter 4). Fix: the matrix built from the business's own history, the opening provision posted with its explanation, and the write-off workflow configured and used.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the silent ledger.</b> Symptom: statements never sent; confirmations dreaded; supplier statements filed unread; disputes living in inboxes. Cause: the record hiding from examination — always a symptom of the hygiene it would fail (Chapter 5). Fix: the hygiene first (Chapters 2-4), then the routines switched on in order — Process Statement Of Accounts, supplier reconciliations, then the confirmation posture.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the immortal advance.</b> Symptom: supplier advances aging past every plausible delivery; the schedule nobody reviews; the advance paid twice. Cause: advances anchored to no expected date and no monthly read (Chapter 3). Fix: advances paid against Purchase Orders, the monthly aging in the routine, and the pull-advances-before-settlement habit audited.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the deleted debtor.</b> Symptom: customers who vanish from the ledger with their balances; a record that cannot answer for its own past. Cause: deletion used where the write-off belonged — completeness broken. Fix: the write-off as the only exit for a debt, records retained always.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: run the tie check end to end including the Journal Entry filter; clear floating items in Payment Reconciliation and state the three resolutions; capture and consume advances in both directions through the order and the invoice's Advances section, and state the never-net rule; size a provision from the AR report's buckets and post the adjusting entry; run a write-off on the two-signature workflow and receive a recovery; schedule Process Statement Of Accounts and reconcile a supplier statement; load openings per-invoice with the Opening Invoice Creation Tool and prove the migration; and run the seven-step month-end routine.</p><p><b>The fourth law: the record is kept real, collectable, current and complete.</b> Module 5 covers the tax accounts and Nigerian compliance.</p>"),
]

